---
source: crates/trousseau-cli/tests/help.rs
expression: stdout
---
Print a key's value.

Writes the raw value to stdout. On a terminal a newline is added after text values, and binary
values are refused (use `--out` or a pipe). When piped, the bytes are written exactly as stored.

Usage: trousseau get [OPTIONS] <KEY>

Arguments:
  <KEY>
          The key to read

Options:
      --out <PATH>
          Write the value to this file instead of stdout

      --force
          Overwrite `--out` if it already exists

      --clip
          Copy the value to the clipboard instead of printing it

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

Global options:
      --store <PATH>
          Use this store file. Wins over `--global` and over store discovery
          
          [env: TROUSSEAU_STORE=]

      --global
          Use your personal store instead of the nearest project store (the `.trousseau` file in
          this directory or a parent)

      --identity <PATH>
          An identity (private key) file to try when unlocking. Repeatable. Also tried:
          `TROUSSEAU_IDENTITY_FILE`, the config file's `identity.files`, the default identity file,
          `~/.ssh/id_ed25519` and `~/.ssh/id_rsa`

      --passphrase-file <PATH>
          Read the store's passphrase from this file instead of prompting. One trailing newline is
          stripped

      --json
          Print one JSON document on stdout instead of text. Errors become a JSON object on stderr

      --quiet
          Suppress informational messages on stderr. Errors still print

      --no-input
          Never prompt. A command that would prompt fails instead: exit 4 for a secret, exit 2 for a
          confirmation

Examples:
  Print a secret:
    trousseau get database/password

  Copy it to the clipboard (cleared again after 45 seconds):
    trousseau get api/token --clip

  Write a binary value to a file (created with mode 0600):
    trousseau get tls/server.key --out server.key

  Use a secret in another command without printing it:
    PGPASSWORD="$(trousseau get db/password)" psql -h db.internal app

  Value plus metadata as JSON:
    trousseau get database/password --json

Notes:
  A missing key exits with code 5. 'trousseau ls' lists the keys.
  To hand many secrets to a program at once, see 'trousseau run'.
